.TH I2C 3
.SH NAME
i2c \- the Raspberry Pi 4's camera and display I2C bus
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #J /dev

.B /dev/i2c0ctl
.B /dev/i2c0data
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The device serves BSC0 on GPIO 44 and 45, the bus on the Pi 4's two
ribbon connectors, as a polled master at 400 kHz with 7-bit addresses.
.PP
Writing
.BI addr " n
to
.B i2c0ctl
sets the device to talk to, and
.BI subaddress " n
how many bytes of register address it takes, 0 to 4. Reading gives them.
.PP
Reads and writes of
.B i2c0data
are transfers with the device. With a subaddress, the file offset is
the register, sent high byte first; a read sends it, stops, and reads.
An address nothing answers is the error
.LR "no device" .
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/bcm2711/devi2c.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR camera (3),
.IR gpio (3)
.SH BUGS
The VideoCore polls a DSI panel's touch controller on this bus and
there is no way to take turns. With a panel attached a transfer can
fail, and a read or a write can be silently wrong: a program that
matters reads its writes back.
